        "reviewed_sha": "735c9f916b1c04b73ec6eb54451c1628354eca4d",
        "evidence": "Proof prose is concrete and reads aloud.",
        "notes": "Separate (non-trap) findings, recomputed. (1) WRONG CHARACTER: \"the hook character chi_{[2,2]}(sigma_i) has trace tr = [2]_q - 2 on a single generator\" is false. The [2,2] irrep of H_4 is 2-dimensional with SYT [[1,2],[3,4]] and [[1,3],[2,4]]; for sigma_1 the first has 1,2 in the same ROW (eigenvalue q) and the second has them in the same COLUMN (eigenvalue -q^-1), so the trace is q - q^-1 = h = 0.2090602988 at q_0, not [2]_q - 2 = 0.0108968667, a factor of 19.2. The classical S_4 value chi_{[2,2]}(transposition) = 0 is reproduced by BOTH expressions at q = 1, which is presumably how the error survived. The sign argument built on it (\"the -2 subtracts the symmetric and alternating eigenvalues already counted\") therefore has no support, and h > 0 at q_0 > 1 so it does not deliver a negative sign either. (2) ACCURACY FRAMING / CALIBRATION PASS-THROUGH: the quoted percentages are errors on the TOTAL ATOMIC MASS, of which the binding energy (the part the framework supplies) is under 1 percent: B/M = 0.694% for Be-9 and 0.824% for C-12. Converting: Be-9 0.192% to 0.108% of mass is 16.10 to 9.05 MeV, i.e. 27.7% to 15.6% of its 58.17 MeV binding; C-12 0.081% to 0.002% is 9.05 to 0.22 MeV, i.e. 9.8% to 0.24% of its 92.16 MeV binding. A \"0.072% mean\" headline is roughly 120x smaller than the corresponding error on the framework-supplied quantity. (3) Under the absolute-mass excision (AGENTS.md, 2026-08-11) an atomic mass in MeV is not a framework prediction, so these should be restated as binding-energy accuracy. (4) tr_M is written here as sum_lambda w_lambda tr_lambda / dim_lambda, colliding with the y_lambda family used by sibling blocks (notation register warns y/w/tilde-w are distinct at q != 1). (5) Arithmetic that DOES check: abs(beta_4) times alpha = 0.083 x 3.74 = 0.3104 MeV."
      }
